third_party holds vendored code from upstream projects. Each directory is
sourced from a single project at a pinned commit (recorded in upstream.pins)
and carries its own attribution headers and licence entry in NOTICE.

This file records what was vendored, what was skipped, and what was changed
— so the next upstream bump is a merge against the pin, not an excavation.

w32/
  Source
    v3/pkg/w32/ in https://github.com/wailsapp/wails
    at commit 0f6083a580df85cc636fe5f3ee2f543eaff9077a
    (pinned in upstream.pins as `wails`).

    These are the Win32 bindings Wails v3 uses, originally from the Winc
    and W32 projects. They reach Win32 through syscall.NewLazyDLL and
    golang.org/x/sys/windows — no cgo.

  Licence
    MIT. Recorded in NOTICE under the Wails entry. The file headers
    preserve the Winc and W32 Authors copyrights alongside Wails'.

  Skipped
    idroptarget.go
      Depends on Wails' webview2 COM bridge
      (github.com/wailsapp/wails/v3/internal/webview2/pkg/combridge),
      which Facet does not vendor. OLE drag-and-drop is out of scope
      until a platform assignment needs it. ole32.go was modified to
      remove RegisterDragDrop and RevokeDragDrop for the same reason.

    gdi32_windows_test.go
    menu_windows_test.go
    screen_cursorfail_test.go
    theme_test.go
      Test files. The bindings themselves are exercised through Facet's
      own platform tests; the upstream tests cover Wails-specific
      integration paths.

  Modified
    constants.go
      Added MK_LBUTTON, MK_RBUTTON, MK_SHIFT, MK_CONTROL, MK_MBUTTON,
      MK_XBUTTON1, MK_XBUTTON2 (mouse key state flags for WM_MOUSE*
      messages) and WHEEL_DELTA (the wheel delta for one notch). Wails
      does not define these because its webview handles mouse input;
      Facet's wndproc needs them to translate WM_MOUSEWHEEL and
      WM_XBUTTONDOWN.

      Also added WM_IME_STARTCOMPOSITION, WM_IME_COMPOSITION and
      WM_IME_ENDCOMPOSITION, for the same reason: Wails' webview owns IME
      composition itself and never needed these.

    imm32.go
      Not from Wails at all — added whole. IMM32 (ImmGetContext,
      ImmReleaseContext, ImmGetCompositionStringW) has no counterpart
      anywhere in Wails v3's w32 package, because its webview-based input
      model never reads a composition string. Facet needs it to draw IME
      composition text inline (platform.IMECompositionEvent). Written in
      this package's own file-per-DLL convention and reached through
      syscall.NewLazyDLL like everything else here, so raw Win32 access
      stays centralised in one place rather than duplicated in platform.

    user32.go
      Added procShowCursor and the ShowCursor wrapper, and
      procAdjustWindowRectExForDpi with the AdjustWindowRectExForDpi
      wrapper. Wails does not expose ShowCursor because its webview
      manages cursor visibility; Facet's cursor backend needs it for
      application-wide cursor visibility. Wails does not need
      AdjustWindowRectExForDpi because its webview handles its own
      sizing; Facet's window creation needs it to compute the outer
      window size from a wanted client size at the correct per-monitor
      DPI, rather than assuming the primary display's scale.

      Also added IsIconic, alongside the existing IsZoomed. Wails'
      webview never needs to distinguish a minimized window from a
      restored one; Facet's platform.Window.State() does.

    ole32.go
      Removed RegisterDragDrop and RevokeDragDrop, along with the
      combridge import. They depended on Wails' webview2 COM bridge,
      which Facet does not vendor. OLE drag-and-drop is out of scope
      until a platform assignment needs it.

mainthread/
  Source
    v3/pkg/application/mainthread_windows.go and mainthread.go in
    https://github.com/wailsapp/wails
    at commit 0f6083a580df85cc636fe5f3ee2f543eaff9077a
    (pinned in upstream.pins as `wails`).

    The hidden-window dispatch technique: PostMessage to a thread queue
    can be swallowed by a modal inner loop, but PostMessage to a window
    is delivered when the modal loop pumps messages. This is the bug
    Wails hit in v2 and fixed with this approach.

  Licence
    MIT. Recorded in NOTICE under the Wails entry.

  Modified
    The Wails code is a method on windowsApp that shares global state
    with the rest of the application package. This is a standalone
    package with its own Dispatcher type, so the platform backend owns
    the instance rather than reaching into a global. The technique and
    the citations are unchanged.

    The hidden window is created in New, matching upstream's
    initMainLoop. Upstream's comment says it directly:

      initMainLoop must be called with the same OSThread that is used
      to call runMainLoop() later.

    Run enforces this with a panic — "Run must be called on the same
    goroutine that called New" — as upstream does. A clear panic beats
    a silent hang, which is what happens when PostMessage posts to a
    window whose thread never pumps messages.

    New locks the OS thread permanently (no defer unlock), rather than
    for the duration of the call as upstream does. Upstream calls
    initMainLoop and runMainLoop in quick succession from main, so the
    goroutine stays on the same thread between them. Facet's tests run
    sequentially with goroutines yielding between them, so the thread
    can be reused. A permanent lock ensures the goroutine that
    constructs the platform stays on the thread that owns the hidden
    window for the platform's lifetime.

    Dispatch runs f immediately when called from the main thread,
    matching upstream's dispatchOnMainThread, which checks
    invokeRequired and runs directly when not required. This is what
    makes NewWindow work before Run: the caller is already on the
    platform thread, so the window is created synchronously without
    needing the loop to pump.
